{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-23:101\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The reckoning horizon also marks a rupture in ordinary kinship: after the trumpet, there are no family ties between them and no mutual questioning.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"23:100\",\"ar\":\"وَمِن وَرَآئِهِم بَرْزَخٌ إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ يُبْعَثُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"23:101\",\"ar\":\"فَإِذَا نُفِخَ فِى ٱلصُّورِ فَلَآ أَنسَابَ بَيْنَهُمْ يَوْمَئِذٍۢ وَلَا يَتَسَآءَلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names a specific Day of Din, and these neighboring verses place the trumpet and resurrection before that day’s social scene.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not call this scene Yawm al-Din or explain why kinship and questioning cease.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:102\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The same resurrection scene pictures a favorable outcome through heavy scales: those whose scales weigh heavily are the successful.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"23:102\",\"ar\":\"فَمَن ثَقُلَتْ مَوَٰزِينُهُۥ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ هُمُ ٱلْمُفْلِحُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus calls the day one of din; this passage supplies a measured outcome for those brought into its final scene.\",\"limits\":\"This verse does not use dīn or name the day; its link to the focus rests on the surrounding resurrection passage.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-23:103\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The opposite scale outcome is loss of the self and lasting residence in Hell, giving the final reckoning a sharply divided result.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"23:103\",\"ar\":\"وَمَنْ خَفَّتْ مَوَٰزِينُهُۥ فَأُو۟لَٰٓئِكَ ٱلَّذِينَ خَسِرُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُمْ فِى جَهَنَّمَ خَٰلِدُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s named Day of Din is placed in a wider resurrection scene where light scales have an explicit adverse consequence.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not identify its day by the focus’s formula; the scene connection comes from its placement after the trumpet passage.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:2\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Here dīn names a divine religious order within which punishment is administered, and the verse explicitly couples that order to belief in the Last Day.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"24:2\",\"ar\":\"فِى دِينِ ٱللَّهِ إِن كُنتُمْ تُؤْمِنُونَ بِٱللَّهِ وَٱلْيَوْمِ ٱلْءَاخِرِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus combines dīn with a day; this verse places the same root in a legal setting and mentions the Last Day in the same clause.\",\"limits\":\"The phrase fī dīn Allāh does not itself mean the Day of Recompense, so the shared root does not establish identical senses.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:25\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"This verse makes dīn an amount God fully renders to people on that day, giving the focus’s Yawm al-Din a direct parallel in enacted recompense.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"24:25\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَئِذٍۢ يُوَفِّيهِمُ ٱللَّهُ دِينَهُمُ ٱلْحَقَّ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both passages join a day to the root d-y-n; here the day’s action is that God fully pays their dīn.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not use the exact construct Yawm al-Din; it shows a close root and scene parallel, not a grammatical equation.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-24:42\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s title over one specific day sits within a broader assertion that all heavenly and earthly dominion belongs to God, to whom all return.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"24:42\",\"ar\":\"وَلِلَّهِ مُلْكُ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ ۖ وَإِلَى ٱللَّهِ ٱلْمَصِيرُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus uses مَٰلِكِ for God’s authority over a day; this verse pairs the related dominion term مُلْك with the return to God.\",\"limits\":\"It does not specify the Day of Din or describe its judgment scene.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:2\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"God’s dominion is presented as exclusive and extending to all creation, which broadens the scope of the focus’s ownership title.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"25:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلَّذِى لَهُۥ مُلْكُ ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضِ وَلَمْ يَتَّخِذْ وَلَدًۭا وَلَمْ يَكُن لَّهُۥ شَرِيكٌۭ فِى ٱلْمُلْكِ وَخَلَقَ كُلَّ شَىْءٍۢ فَقَدَّرَهُۥ تَقْدِيرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"مَٰلِكِ in the focus shares the dominion root with this claim of sole ملك and measured creation.\",\"limits\":\"This passage does not mention a day, return, or reckoning, so it broadens sovereignty without specifying the focus’s temporal scene.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-25:26\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s ownership of the Day is echoed as ملك on that very day, declared to belong rightfully to al-Raḥmān.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"25:26\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْمُلْكُ يَوْمَئِذٍ ٱلْحَقُّ لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared يوم and the dominion root make this a close parallel; the title al-Raḥmān also recalls the preceding divine name in the Fatiha.\",\"limits\":\"This verse uses مُلْك rather than مَٰلِك and does not use dīn; it states rightful sovereignty, not the full contents of the reckoning.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:35\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:38\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:82\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Abraham’s hope for forgiveness on Yawm al-Din presents that day as a horizon for appeal to mercy as well as accountability.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:82\",\"ar\":\"وَٱلَّذِىٓ أَطْمَعُ أَن يَغْفِرَ لِى خَطِيٓـَٔتِى يَوْمَ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"activation\":\"This is the exact Day of Din formula from the focus, now spoken by a servant who hopes for forgiveness.\",\"limits\":\"The verse gives Abraham’s hope, not a guarantee of the outcome or a full account of the day’s proceedings.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-26:156\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"A threatened عَذَابُ يَوْمٍ عَظِيمٍ in the she-camel episode shows that a day of punishment can refer to a community’s worldly destruction, distinct from the focus’s named Day of Din.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"26:155\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ هَٰذِهِۦ نَاقَةٌۭ لَّهَا شِرْبٌۭ وَلَكُمْ شِرْبُ يَوْمٍۢ مَّعْلُومٍ\"},{\"ref\":\"26:156\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تَمَسُّوهَا بِسُوٓءٍۢ فَيَأْخُذَكُمْ عَذَابُ يَوْمٍ عَظِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The shared day-and-punishment language invites comparison, while the she-camel’s allotted drinking day locates this warning in the story’s earthly setting.\",\"limits\":\"The passage does not connect this punishment to the final reckoning or use dīn.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:70\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s ownership and reckoning cohere here as God’s الحكم and the return of all people to Him.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"28:70\",\"ar\":\"وَلَهُ ٱلْحُكْمُ وَإِلَيْهِ تُرْجَعُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus assigns the Day to God; this verse joins judgment authority with the return that brings people before Him.\",\"limits\":\"ٱلْحُكْمُ is not the focus’s word dīn, and the verse does not name a specific day.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-28:71\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Yawm al-Qiyāma appears as the endpoint in a hypothetical of perpetual night, confirming a final temporal horizon without depicting judgment itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"28:71\",\"ar\":\"إِن جَعَلَ ٱللَّهُ عَلَيْكُمُ ٱلَّيْلَ سَرْمَدًا إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The resurrection-day phrase follows the adjacent declaration of God’s judgment and the return to Him.\",\"limits\":\"In this verse the day marks the duration of a hypothetical night, not a scene of reckoning or the meaning of dīn.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-29:65\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The shipwrecked call on God with undivided dīn, then return to shirk when rescued; alongside the claim that the Hereafter is the true life, this exposes devotion that lasts only until danger passes.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"29:64\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّ ٱلدَّارَ ٱلْءَاخِرَةَ لَهِىَ ٱلْحَيَوَانُ\"},{\"ref\":\"29:65\",\"ar\":\"دَعَوُا۟ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصِينَ لَهُ ٱلدِّينَ فَلَمَّا نَجَّىٰهُمْ إِلَى ٱلْبَرِّ إِذَا هُمْ يُشْرِكُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the final day; this passage contrasts temporary crisis-devotion with the enduring horizon of the Hereafter.\",\"limits\":\"Here dīn is devotion, not the day’s recompense, so the relationship is ethical and temporal rather than a shared phrase.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:12\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"When the Hour rises, the criminals are struck dumb with despair, supplying an experiential response to the final day that the focus leaves unstated.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"30:12\",\"ar\":\"وَيَوْمَ تَقُومُ ٱلسَّاعَةُ يُبْلِسُ ٱلْمُجْرِمُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the day of final accounting; this verse shows the criminals’ response at the Hour’s arrival.\",\"limits\":\"It names the Hour rather than Yawm al-Din and does not describe the accounting itself.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:30\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Here dīn is the upright way aligned with God’s created fitrah, raising the possibility that Yawm al-Din can evoke judgment of a lived order as well as repayment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"30:30\",\"ar\":\"فَأَقِمْ وَجْهَكَ لِلدِّينِ حَنِيفًۭا ۚ فِطْرَتَ ٱللَّهِ ٱلَّتِى فَطَرَ ٱلنَّاسَ عَلَيْهَا ۚ لَا تَبْدِيلَ لِخَلْقِ ٱللَّهِ ۚ ذَٰلِكَ ٱلدِّينُ ٱلْقَيِّمُ\"}],\"missing\":\"A passage explicitly linking the final day to judgment of the upright dīn, or a local cue in 1:4 that selects this normative sense, would establish the proposed breadth; the focus’s construct currently favors reckoning.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-30:43\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Upright dīn is to be established before an irreversible day from God, when people divide; the final horizon gives present religious orientation urgency.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"30:43\",\"ar\":\"فَأَقِمْ وَجْهَكَ لِلدِّينِ ٱلْقَيِّمِ مِن قَبْلِ أَن يَأْتِىَ يَوْمٌۭ لَّا مَرَدَّ لَهُۥ مِنَ ٱللَّهِ ۖ يَوْمَئِذٍۢ يَصَّدَّعُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"This verse places dīn and an irreversible day in one sequence, echoing the focus’s pairing of dīn with a day under God’s authority.\",\"limits\":\"It calls the way al-dīn al-qayyim and the event an unnamed day, rather than using Yawm al-Din.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-31:32\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The storm forces people to call on God with sincere dīn, and the next verse warns of a day when parent and child cannot answer for one another; the sequence moves from present dependence to final accountability.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"31:32\",\"ar\":\"دَعَوُا۟ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصِينَ لَهُ ٱلدِّينَ فَلَمَّا نَجَّىٰهُمْ إِلَى ٱلْبَرِّ\"},{\"ref\":\"31:33\",\"ar\":\"وَٱخْشَوْا۟ يَوْمًۭا لَّا يَجْزِى وَالِدٌ عَن وَلَدِهِۦ وَلَا مَوْلُودٌ هُوَ جَازٍ عَن وَالِدِهِۦ شَيْـًٔا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s day of dīn and the Fatiha’s following pledge of worship meet this passage’s contrast between crisis-driven devotion and a day no kinship can avert.\",\"limits\":\"The dīn in 31:32 means devotion, and 31:33 does not label its day Yawm al-Din.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-33:56\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-34:30\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The promised day is fixed so completely that no one can delay or advance it by an hour; the following scene places deniers before their Lord.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"34:30\",\"ar\":\"قُل لَّكُم مِّيعَادُ يَوْمٍۢ لَّا تَسْتَـْٔخِرُونَ عَنْهُ سَاعَةًۭ وَلَا تَسْتَقْدِمُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"34:31\",\"ar\":\"وَلَوْ تَرَىٰٓ إِذِ ٱلظَّٰلِمُونَ مَوْقُوفُونَ عِندَ رَبِّهِمْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus assigns the day to its owner; this passage stresses that its appointed time cannot be manipulated.\",\"limits\":\"It does not call the appointment Yawm al-Din or describe its full reckoning.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-36:51\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The final horizon unfolds through the trumpet and emergence from graves toward the Lord, before the later declaration that deeds alone determine recompense.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"36:51\",\"ar\":\"وَنُفِخَ فِى ٱلصُّورِ فَإِذَا هُم مِّنَ ٱلْأَجْدَاثِ إِلَىٰ رَبِّهِمْ يَنسِلُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"36:54\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا تُجْزَوْنَ إِلَّا مَا كُنتُمْ تَعْمَلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the Day of Din; this passage supplies both the return-to-Lord scene and its rule of recompense.\",\"limits\":\"The target verse itself describes emergence, not the weighing or judgment that follows.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-36:54\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The day’s governing rule is that no self is wronged and each is recompensed only for what it did, specifying the justice implied by din.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"36:54\",\"ar\":\"فَٱلْيَوْمَ لَا تُظْلَمُ نَفْسٌۭ شَيْـًۭٔا وَلَا تُجْزَوْنَ إِلَّا مَا كُنتُمْ تَعْمَلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"This verse follows the trumpet and resurrection sequence, then explicitly states the day’s recompense rule.\",\"limits\":\"It does not use the noun dīn or assign the title مَٰلِكِ, though its scene directly concerns the focus’s day.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:20\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The resurrected themselves recognize the event as Yawm al-Din, but do so in a cry of woe, making recognition part of the day’s scene.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"37:20\",\"ar\":\"وَقَالُوا۟ يَٰوَيْلَنَا هَٰذَا يَوْمُ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"activation\":\"This is the focus’s exact day formula voiced by those who had denied it.\",\"limits\":\"Their cry identifies the day but does not explain its proceedings or name its sovereign.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:21\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The same day is named Yawm al-Faṣl, so the focus’s dīn horizon includes a decisive separation that deniers had rejected.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"37:20\",\"ar\":\"هَٰذَا يَوْمُ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"37:21\",\"ar\":\"هَٰذَا يَوْمُ ٱلْفَصْلِ ٱلَّذِى كُنتُم بِهِۦ تُكَذِّبُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The neighboring verses repeat هَٰذَا يَوْمُ, defining the first name with a second and linking both to prior denial.\",\"limits\":\"The two labels are juxtaposed, not glossed as fully interchangeable in every respect.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:24\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The gathered wrongdoers are stopped because they must answer questions, presenting the day as an interrogative account, not simply a sentence pronounced.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"37:24\",\"ar\":\"وَقِفُوهُمْ ۖ إِنَّهُم مَّسْـُٔولُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"After the day is called both Yawm al-Din and Yawm al-Faṣl, the command to halt them shows one action within that reckoning.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not specify what questions are asked or state that every person undergoes this same scene.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:53\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The related form لَمَدِينُونَ voices the question of whether the dead will indeed be brought under requital, giving the focus’s dīn a concrete sense of due and accountability.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"37:53\",\"ar\":\"أَءِذَا مِتْنَا وَكُنَّا تُرَابًۭا وَعِظَٰمًا أَءِنَّا لَمَدِينُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse shares the d-y-n root with ٱلدِّينِ and places it in a question about life after death.\",\"limits\":\"This is a different form from the focus’s noun, and the question is voiced skeptically rather than answered here.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-37:155\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-38:78\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Satan’s curse extends to Yawm al-Din, while his request immediately afterward seeks delay until resurrection; evil’s time is bounded by the same final horizon.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"38:78\",\"ar\":\"وَإِنَّ عَلَيْكَ لَعْنَتِىٓ إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"38:79\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ رَبِّ فَأَنظِرْنِىٓ إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ يُبْعَثُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s exact Day of Din phrase is repeated as the endpoint of the curse, then echoed by Satan’s request for respite.\",\"limits\":\"The verses do not narrate Satan’s fate on that day or state that the requested respite lasts until the curse’s endpoint.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-38:81\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The granted respite ends at Yawm al-Waqt al-Maʿlūm; the preceding request asks for delay until resurrection, leaving open whether this is the same endpoint as Yawm al-Din.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"38:79\",\"ar\":\"إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ يُبْعَثُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"38:81\",\"ar\":\"إِلَىٰ يَوْمِ ٱلْوَقْتِ ٱلْمَعْلُومِ\"}],\"missing\":\"An explicit Quranic identification of the known appointed time with the day of resurrection or Yawm al-Din would settle whether the two endpoints coincide.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The command to worship Allah with undivided dīn forms a present response to the focus’s future horizon; the Fatiha itself moves from the Day of Din directly to إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"},{\"ref\":\"39:2\",\"ar\":\"فَٱعْبُدِ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصًۭا لَّهُ ٱلدِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both the Fatiha’s sequence and 39:2 put exclusive worship next to the focus’s concern with dīn.\",\"limits\":\"Dīn in 39:2 denotes sincere devotion; the verse does not itself say this devotion is motivated by the final day.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The verse joins Allah’s exclusive claim to dīn with His judging between disputants, linking present religious allegiance to divine adjudication.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:3\",\"ar\":\"أَلَا لِلَّهِ ٱلدِّينُ ٱلْخَالِصُ\"},{\"ref\":\"39:3\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ يَحْكُمُ بَيْنَهُمْ فِى مَا هُمْ فِيهِ يَخْتَلِفُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus places Allah’s ownership over dīn’s day; here sole possession of dīn and judgment over disagreement occur together.\",\"limits\":\"The verse does not name the day, and dīn al-khāliṣ here is not a direct grammatical gloss of Yawm al-Din.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:11\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The prophet states exclusive worship as a command laid upon him, recasting sincere dīn as an enacted obligation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:11\",\"ar\":\"قُلْ إِنِّىٓ أُمِرْتُ أَنْ أَعْبُدَ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصًۭا لَّهُ ٱلدِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Like the focus’s following pledge of worship, this verse brings dīn into a first-person statement of service to God.\",\"limits\":\"It repeats the devotional sense and command of 39:2 without adding a day or final-reckoning scene.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:14\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The speaker’s exclusive worship is set between fear of a great day and the warning of loss on Resurrection Day, joining present dīn to its final consequence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:13\",\"ar\":\"إِنْ عَصَيْتُ رَبِّى عَذَابَ يَوْمٍ عَظِيمٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"39:14\",\"ar\":\"قُلِ ٱللَّهَ أَعْبُدُ مُخْلِصًۭا لَّهُۥ دِينِى\"},{\"ref\":\"39:15\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ ٱلْخَٰسِرِينَ ٱلَّذِينَ خَسِرُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُمْ وَأَهْلِيهِمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The surrounding sequence places dīn between a threatened day and a named day of loss, echoing the focus’s connection of dīn and day.\",\"limits\":\"Dīn in the middle verse is the speaker’s devotion, not a name for the day itself.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:68\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The final scene begins with two trumpet blasts: the first brings death, the second makes people stand looking, before the book and judgment appear.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:68\",\"ar\":\"وَنُفِخَ فِى ٱلصُّورِ فَصَعِقَ مَن فِى ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَمَن فِى ٱلْأَرْضِ إِلَّا مَن شَآءَ ٱللَّهُ ۖ ثُمَّ نُفِخَ فِيهِ أُخْرَىٰ فَإِذَا هُمْ قِيَامٌۭ يَنظُرُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"39:69\",\"ar\":\"وَوُضِعَ ٱلْكِتَٰبُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the final Day of Din; these adjacent verses provide its event sequence from trumpet to standing before judgment.\",\"limits\":\"Verse 68 is the resurrection stage, not the reckoning itself.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:69\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The reckoning is staged with a book, prophets, witnesses, and judgment in truth, with an explicit assurance that no one is wronged.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:69\",\"ar\":\"وَوُضِعَ ٱلْكِتَٰبُ وَجِا۟ىٓءَ بِٱلنَّبِيِّۦنَ وَٱلشُّهَدَآءِ وَقُضِىَ بَيْنَهُم بِٱلْحَقِّ وَهُمْ لَا يُظْلَمُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"This verse immediately follows the trumpet and standing scene and gives institutional detail to the focus’s Day of Din.\",\"limits\":\"It does not use the word dīn or the title مَٰلِكِ, although judgment is explicit.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-39:70\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Every self is paid in full for what it did, making the day’s din an exhaustive settlement with deeds.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"39:70\",\"ar\":\"وَوُفِّيَتْ كُلُّ نَفْسٍۢ مَّا عَمِلَتْ وَهُوَ أَعْلَمُ بِمَا يَفْعَلُونَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The verse completes the preceding court scene by stating what happens to each person under judgment.\",\"limits\":\"It describes the outcome but does not label the scene Yawm al-Din.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-40:14\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Exclusive dīn is commanded immediately before a warning of Yawm al-Talāq, connecting present devotion to the coming encounter with God.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"40:14\",\"ar\":\"فَٱدْعُوا۟ ٱللَّهَ مُخْلِصِينَ لَهُ ٱلدِّينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"40:15\",\"ar\":\"لِيُنذِرَ يَوْمَ ٱلتَّلَاقِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s Day of Din and this passage’s Day of Encounter share a future horizon, while the adjacent command supplies a present response.\",\"limits\":\"Yawm al-Talāq is not explicitly identified here as Yawm al-Din.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-40:16\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The day’s sovereignty is publicly reclaimed through the question لِمَنِ ٱلْمُلْكُ ٱلْيَوْمَ and its answer, followed by the rule that every self is recompensed.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"40:16\",\"ar\":\"لِّمَنِ ٱلْمُلْكُ ٱلْيَوْمَ ۖ لِلَّهِ ٱلْوَٰحِدِ ٱلْقَهَّارِ\"},{\"ref\":\"40:17\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْيَوْمَ تُجْزَىٰ كُلُّ نَفْسٍۭ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same day holds both the declaration of Allah’s sole dominion and the individual accounting implied by the focus.\",\"limits\":\"This passage uses مُلْك rather than مَٰلِك and does not use dīn, though its scene closely matches the focus’s combined sovereignty and reckoning.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-40:17\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Each self is recompensed for its earning without injustice, and God is swift in account; the verse spells out the justice and speed within the focus’s day.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"40:17\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْيَوْمَ تُجْزَىٰ كُلُّ نَفْسٍۭ بِمَا كَسَبَتْ ۚ لَا ظُلْمَ ٱلْيَوْمَ ۚ إِنَّ ٱللَّهَ سَرِيعُ ٱلْحِسَابِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The neighboring verse asks whose dominion is manifest today and answers that it is Allah’s, making this accounting its immediate consequence.\",\"limits\":\"The verse describes judgment’s rule, not the focus’s exact dīn wording.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-40:32\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The warned-of Yawm al-Tanād may add a scene of calling and abandonment to the focus’s final horizon, but its identity with Yawm al-Din is not stated here.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"40:32\",\"ar\":\"وَيَٰقَوْمِ إِنِّىٓ أَخَافُ عَلَيْكُمْ يَوْمَ ٱلتَّنَادِ\"},{\"ref\":\"40:33\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَ تُوَلُّونَ مُدْبِرِينَ مَا لَكُم مِّنَ ٱللَّهِ مِنْ عَاصِمٍۢ\"}],\"missing\":\"An explicit cross-verse identification of Yawm al-Tanād with Yawm al-Din, or a fuller account of the calling in this scene, could establish what this name adds to the focus.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:13\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Dīn here is a shared order prescribed across prophets; the following verse says disputes over it await judgment at an appointed term, which may connect the focus’s day to the settlement of rival religious claims.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"42:13\",\"ar\":\"شَرَعَ لَكُم مِّنَ ٱلدِّينِ مَا وَصَّىٰ بِهِۦ نُوحًۭا وَٱلَّذِىٓ أَوْحَيْنَآ إِلَيْكَ وَمَا وَصَّيْنَا بِهِۦٓ إِبْرَٰهِيمَ وَمُوسَىٰ وَعِيسَىٰٓ ۖ أَنْ أَقِيمُوا۟ ٱلدِّينَ وَلَا تَتَفَرَّقُوا۟ فِيهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"42:14\",\"ar\":\"وَلَوْلَا كَلِمَةٌۭ سَبَقَتْ مِن رَّبِّكَ إِلَىٰٓ أَجَلٍۢ مُّسَمًّۭى لَّقُضِىَ بَيْنَهُمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"The passage does not explicitly identify the appointed term or the judgment among factions as Yawm al-Din; that equation would turn this parallel into a defined link.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:21\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"This verse questions religious laws claimed without God’s permission and says a separating word delays judgment between the disputants, suggesting that the day of dīn could answer competing claims about dīn.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"42:21\",\"ar\":\"شَرَعُوا۟ لَهُم مِّنَ ٱلدِّينِ مَا لَمْ يَأْذَنۢ بِهِ ٱللَّهُ ۚ وَلَوْلَا كَلِمَةُ ٱلْفَصْلِ لَقُضِىَ بَيْنَهُمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"An explicit identification of كَلِمَةُ ٱلْفَصْلِ or the deferred judgment with Yawm al-Din would establish the timing and settle how the legal sense of dīn relates to the focus.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-42:45\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The final scene makes loss public: wrongdoers are presented before the Fire in humiliation, while believers name the losers as those who lost themselves and their families on Resurrection Day.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"42:45\",\"ar\":\"وَتَرَىٰهُمْ يُعْرَضُونَ عَلَيْهَا خَٰشِعِينَ مِنَ ٱلذُّلِّ يَنظُرُونَ مِن طَرْفٍ خَفِىٍّۢ ۗ وَقَالَ ٱلَّذِينَ ءَامَنُوٓا۟ إِنَّ ٱلْخَٰسِرِينَ ٱلَّذِينَ خَسِرُوٓا۟ أَنفُسَهُمْ وَأَهْلِيهِمْ يَوْمَ ٱلْقِيَٰمَةِ ۗ أَلَآ إِنَّ ٱلظَّٰلِمِينَ فِى عَذَابٍۢ مُّقِيمٍۢ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus names the day of accounting; this passage shows its visible humiliation and lasting loss.\",\"limits\":\"It names Yawm al-Qiyāma rather than Yawm al-Din and does not state the accounting procedure.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-44:9\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The people’s doubt and play are followed by a command to await a day of manifest smoke; the sequence may frame denial as answerable to an appointed punishment.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"44:9\",\"ar\":\"بَلْ هُمْ فِى شَكٍّۢ يَلْعَبُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"44:10\",\"ar\":\"فَٱرْتَقِبْ يَوْمَ تَأْتِى ٱلسَّمَآءُ بِدُخَانٍۢ مُّبِينٍۢ\"}],\"missing\":\"The text here does not establish whether the smoke-day is a worldly punishment or the final Day of Din; a direct identification or further scene details could resolve its relevance.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-44:16\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The great seizure is called a day of divine vengeance, but this passage leaves open whether it is a historical punishment or the final reckoning named in the focus.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"44:16\",\"ar\":\"يَوْمَ نَبْطِشُ ٱلْبَطْشَةَ ٱلْكُبْرَىٰٓ إِنَّا مُنتَقِمُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"44:17\",\"ar\":\"وَلَقَدْ فَتَنَّا قَبْلَهُمْ قَوْمَ فِرْعَوْنَ وَجَآءَهُمْ رَسُولٌۭ كَرِيمٌ\"}],\"missing\":\"A clearer referent for the great seizure, or a passage explicitly equating it with Yawm al-Din, is needed to determine whether it describes the same final event.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-44:40\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The appointed day for all is called Yawm al-Faṣl, giving the focus’s Day of Din a universal, decisive-separation aspect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"44:40\",\"ar\":\"إِنَّ يَوْمَ ٱلْفَصْلِ مِيقَٰتُهُمْ أَجْمَعِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"Both names designate a day set over people; this verse emphasizes its appointment for everyone and its separating function.\",\"limits\":\"It supplies a parallel name and function, not an explicit statement that every aspect of Yawm al-Faṣl and Yawm al-Din is identical.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
